WEBVTT

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:08.630
The fifth conversation is about how we treat each other: how do we want to be together?

00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:10.970
Most organisations have values.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.610
Trust, respect, ownership.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.070
They’re on posters and in slide decks.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:23.210
And almost nobody can tell you what they mean on a Tuesday afternoon, when a deadline is close and someone just missed a meeting.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.970
That’s why this conversation is not about values as words.

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.690
It’s about behaviours you could actually see.

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.270
Not “we respect each other”, but “we let people finish their sentence”.

00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:39.750
Not “we trust each other”, but “we say it to the person, not about the person”.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:44.570
Signs your team needs this conversation: there’s tension that nobody names.

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:47.470
People complain to you, but not to each other.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.410
Some behaviour bothers several people, but everyone tolerates it.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.870
Or new people don’t know the unwritten rules, and keep stepping on them.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:04.789
The output on the canvas is a short team contract, with two parts: the behaviours that belong in this team, and the behaviours that don’t. In and out.

00:01:04.839 --> 00:01:11.610
This conversation is especially valuable after the “Who are we?” conversation, because people already know more about each other.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.950
And it’s especially hard when there is conflict in the team.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:20.664
If the tension is about you, or about one person, think about bringing in someone else to facilitate it.

